22:59:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a very understandable instinct, and it gets to the heart of the tension. Every new device does add a potential entry point—that's true. But I'd ask you to consider a different way of framing it: adding a connected device isn't just adding a risk; it's also adding a sensor, a watchdog, or an automated protector that you didn't have before. Think of it like locks on your doors. Adding a deadbolt doesn't make you feel less safe because it's "another thing that could be picked." Instead, you see it as an additional layer of security that makes unauthorized entry harder. IoT devices can work the same way. A smart smoke detector doesn't just sit there—it can alert your phone if it detects smoke while you're away, and even shut off your oven remotely. That's not spying; that's active protection. The "spying" fear is real, but it's largely a product of opaque data practices from a few bad actors. The solution isn't to reject all connected devices—it's to demand and choose devices that respect your privacy. For example, you can buy smart cameras that store footage locally on an SD card rather than sending it to a cloud server. You can use smart plugs that don't require internet access at all, just local control via Bluetooth. You can disable microphones on voice assistants when not in use. The point is: the technology itself is neutral. It can be a spy or a guardian, depending on how it's designed and used. And the trend is strongly toward giving users more control, not less. So while your caution is healthy, I'd argue that a carefully chosen, privacy-respecting IoT setup can actually give you more peace of mind than a home full of "dumb" devices that offer no awareness or remote control at all. Does that distinction make sense? Would you trust a device more if it had a physical camera shutter and no internet upload capability?
