22:59:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right to push back on that analogy—it's not perfect, and I appreciate you calling it out. A deadbolt is passive; it just sits there. An IoT device is active and often communicative. That's a meaningful difference. But let me refine the point: think of IoT devices more like a security guard who patrols your property and radios you when something's wrong, rather than a lock. Yes, that guard sees things and reports them—but you hired them, you set the rules for what they report, and you can fire them or limit their access at any time. The problem isn't the guard; it's when the guard secretly reports to someone else without your knowledge. So the real question isn't "should I have any guards at all?" but rather "can I trust the guard I hire?" And the answer is increasingly yes—if you choose carefully. Modern IoT platforms let you: - Disable cloud uploads entirely - Set data retention limits (e.g., delete footage after 24 hours) - Use end-to-end encryption so even the manufacturer can't see your data - Physically block cameras or microphones when you want true offline privacy The "constantly watching and sending data out" problem is real for poorly designed devices, but it's not a law of physics—it's a design choice. And the market is shifting toward privacy-first designs because consumers are demanding it. So while your concern is valid, I'd argue it's a problem we can solve, not an inherent flaw of IoT itself. If you could design your ideal IoT device—one that only sends alerts when you want, stores everything locally, and has a physical off switch—would you feel differently about having it in your home?
