22:59:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I'm really sorry that happened to you. That's not a theoretical risk—that's a real, painful failure of a device at the worst possible moment. And it's completely understandable that an experience like that would shake your trust in the entire category. That said, I want to be honest: that failure wasn't because IoT is inherently unreliable; it was because of a specific design flaw in that product—a poor battery management system, a lack of a low-battery warning that actually got your attention, or maybe no wired backup option. Those are real problems, but they're solvable ones. Many modern IoT security devices now offer wired power, battery backups, and proactive alerts that nag you until you recharge. But I also want to acknowledge something deeper: your experience highlights a broader truth about IoT that I should have been more upfront about. IoT devices are not magic; they require maintenance. A deadbolt doesn't need a firmware update or a battery check. An IoT device does. And if a user isn't warned properly, or if the device fails silently, it can create a false sense of security that's worse than having no device at all. So I'll concede this: IoT is not a net positive for everyone in every situation. For someone who doesn't want to manage batteries, check for updates, or configure privacy settings, a simpler non-connected solution may genuinely be safer and more private. The net positive argument depends on informed, engaged users and well-designed products. But I'd still argue that for those who are willing to be thoughtful about it, IoT offers capabilities—remote monitoring, instant alerts, automation—that no dumb device can match. The challenge is making those devices reliable and trustworthy enough that even a bad experience like yours becomes the exception, not the rule. Do you think you'd ever consider a wired, professionally installed IoT security system, or has that experience permanently soured you on the idea?
